Answer: No. Java does not support Multiple Inheritance through classes. A class cannot directly extend two classes.

```java
class Child extends Parent1, Parent2 {
}
```

This is not valid Java syntax.

Answer: Multiple Inheritance through classes can create ambiguity when different parent classes provide members with the same name. For example:

```java
class Parent1 {
String color = "black";
}
class Parent2 {
String color = "white";
}
```

If a child class inherited both color members, it would be unclear which value should be used.

Answer: If two parent classes define a member with the same name, the child class would have multiple possible sources for that member. For example:

```java
class Parent1 {
String color = "black";
}
class Parent2 {
String color = "white";
}
```

The child would have an ambiguity when accessing color.

Answer: A Java class cannot extend two classes.

```java
class Child extends Parent1, Parent2 {
}
```

This causes a compilation error.

